.\" Original maux page, MIT, 2026-09-19.
.\" Historical-source decision: Historical UNIX ld has incompatible object/library formats and options; this is a house Merlin REL linker.
.\" Rendered by make man-pages; staged on play image in /usr/man/.
.TH LD 1 "September 19, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
ld \- link Merlin REL objects
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ld
.RB [ \-a " address" ]
.RB [ \-z ]
.RB [ \-t " type" ]
.BI \-o " output"
.RB [ \-L " directory" ]
.RI [ module " | " \-l " library | " \-s " address ...]"
.br
.B ld
.BI @ file
.SH DESCRIPTION
Link Merlin REL modules, resolving their exported definitions and
external references, and write a flat output file.
Module code lengths are taken from each object's ProDOS auxiliary type;
preserve the file metadata when transporting objects.
.TP
.B \-a address
Set the link origin in hexadecimal, default 0800.
.TP
.B \-o output
Name the output file (required).
.TP
.B \-t type
Set the output ProDOS type byte in hexadecimal, default 06 (BIN).
The link origin becomes its auxiliary type.
.TP
.B \-z
Omit trailing zero bytes from the output. Use only when the program
establishes any required working storage itself.
.TP
.B \-L directory
Add a search directory, before /mnt/MAUX/LIB. Used for
.B \-l
library resolution (unchanged: searched before /usr/lib), and also to
resolve an explicit
.I module
operand that contains no slash: such a module is tried in each
.B \-L
directory, in the order given on the command line, before falling back
to the name exactly as given (relative to the current prefix; unlike
.BR \-l ", " \-L
does not fall back to /usr/lib for a bare module operand). A module
operand containing a slash is never searched; it is opened exactly as
given, by construction, so existing absolute\- or relative\-path
invocations are unaffected by adding
.BR \-L .
The attached spelling \-L/path (one token, no space) is accepted, the
same idiom as
.BR \-s .
It exists because a command line with several modules and one or more
.B \-L
directories can exceed the shell's 32-token argument limit before it
exceeds the 254-character line limit; the attached form removes one
token per
.B \-L
without shortening anything.
.TP
.B \-l library
Search for directory LIBlibrary, selecting REL members needed to satisfy
unresolved names. Each library is rescanned until it adds no members.
Selected members occupy the flag's position among modules, in directory
order. Library order matters.
.TP
.B \-s address
Link following modules at a separate run address while the output file
offset continues. -s 0 ends the run; up to two nonzero runs are
allowed (sh links a segment at E000 and one at D000).
The attached spelling -sE000 is accepted.
.TP
.BI @ file
Read the arguments from
.IR file :
its words, separated by blanks and line ends (either ASCII convention),
replace the command line, and every option above works in it.
.BI @ file
must be the only operand. A shell command line is limited to 31
arguments, which
.BR make (1)
and
.BR sh (1)
share; the native build links sh, vi, more, ex, nulib2 and diff through
an argument file (up to 99 words and 1022 bytes). An unreadable or
oversized file is refused with a diagnostic and status 1.
.PP
Options normally take separate operands. Addresses have hexadecimal digits
without $ or 0x prefixes.
A library is a ProDOS directory, not a Unix ar archive.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/LD  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
nas(1), as(1), nm(1), make(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 on success, 1 for link/I/O failures, 2 for usage errors.
.SH BUGS
Limits include 96 total modules, four -L directories, four -l requests, a 3584-byte dictionary tail per object and a 16384-byte symbol-name pool. A pass-two failure attempts to remove partial output; do not use a pre-existing output as proof of a successful link. Supplied on native toolchain images.
